{"records":[{"id":"t98pjcd7t6","title":"Single-cell lineage tracing of metastatic cancer reveals selection of hybrid EMT states","description":"The underpinnings of cancer metastasis remain poorly understood, in part due to a lack of tools for probing their emergence at high resolution. Here we present macsGESTALT, an inducible CRISPR-Cas9-based lineage recorder with highly efficient single-cell capture of both transcriptional and phylogenetic information. Applying macsGESTALT to a mouse model of metastatic pancreatic cancer, we recover ~380,000 CRISPR target sites and reconstruct dissemination of ~28,000 single cells across multiple metastatic sites. We find cells occupy a continuum of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) states. Metastatic potential peaks in rare, late-hybrid EMT states, which are aggressively selected from a predominately epithelial ancestral pool. The gene signatures of these late-hybrid EMT states are predictive of reduced survival in both human pancreatic and lung cancer patients, highlighting their relevance to clinical disease progression. Finally, we observe evidence for in vivo propagation of S100 family gene expression across clonally distinct metastatic subpopulations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-29T09:16:44.113Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t98pjcd7t6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t98pjcd7t6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamen","last_name":"Simeonov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Pancreatic Cancer","Metastasis","S100A Gene","Cell Lineage","Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xms4mt7j54","title":"CoPEC and chemotherapeutic drugs resistance","description":"Colibactin-producing E. coli (CoPEC) increase colorectal tumor development in mice. Furthermore they also induce mutations which are found in patients' tumors. Here we investigated the impact of CoPEC on chemotherapeutic drugs resistance. For that we used in vitro models as well as in vivo (xenograft and AOM/DSS).\nWe analyzed the expression of proteins known to be involved in drug resistance (proteins involved in epithelial mesenchymal transition as well as cancer stem cell). We found an up-regulation of those proteins after CoPEC infection. Data were validated in vivo. Together our results suggest that CoPEC might increase chemotherapeutic drugs resistance.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-16T16:51:21.792Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xms4mt7j54","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xms4mt7j54"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Guillaume","last_name":"Dalmasso","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Chemotherapy","Cancer Stem Cell","Colorectal Cancer","Escherichia coli","Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dwf7ccphpz","title":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors inhibit pan-cancer epithelial-mesenchymal transition by targeting XBP1+ mast cells","description":"HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors inhibit pan-cancer epithelial-mesenchymal transition by targeting XBP1+ mast cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-24T09:11:41.025Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dwf7ccphpz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dwf7ccphpz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lisheng","last_name":"Peng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer Immunology","Mast Cell"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pt3gmyk5r2","title":"Mass cytometric and transcriptomic profiling of epithelial-mesenchymal transitions in human mammary cell lines","description":"Epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT) equips breast cancer cells for metastasis and treatment resistance. Detection, inhibition, and elimination of cells that are undergoing EMT is challenging due to the intrinsic heterogeneity of cancer cells and the phenotypic diversity of EMT programs. We comprehensively profiled EMT transition phenotypes in four non-cancerous human mammary epithelial cell lines using a FACS surface-marker screen, RNA sequencing, and mass cytometry. EMT was induced in the HMLE and MCF10A cell lines and in the HMLE-Twist-ER and HMLE-Snail-ER cell lines by prolonged exposure to TGFβ1 or 4-hydroxytamoxifen, respectively. Each cell line exhibited a spectrum of EMT transition phenotypes, which were compared to steady-state phenotypes of fifteen luminal and basal breast cancer cell lines. Our data provide multiparametric insights at the single-cell level into the phenotypic diversity of EMT at different time points for the four human cellular models, shedding light on the complexity of EMT and enabling comparisons of EMT transitions between cellular models and design of EMT time course experiments.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-10T13:24:29.638Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pt3gmyk5r2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pt3gmyk5r2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Johanna","last_name":"Wagner","orcid":"0000-0002-5087-3657"},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Masek","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrea","last_name":"Jacobs","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Charlotte","last_name":"Soneson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sujana","last_name":"Sivapatham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nicolas","last_name":"Damond","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Natalie","last_name":"de Souza","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mark D.","last_name":"Robinson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bernd","last_name":"Bodenmiller","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["RNA Sequencing","Cell Culture","Epithelial Cell","Flow Cytometry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mtm2s3hksp","title":"Detecting epithelial-mesenchymal transition signaling molecules in cervical epithelial cells aids in the early diagnosis of cervical lesions","description":"Raw data of \"Detecting epithelial-mesenchymal transition signaling molecules in cervical epithelial cells aids in the early diagnosis of cervical lesions\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-05T19:42:47.487Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mtm2s3hksp","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mtm2s3hksp"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhanfeng","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Guangdong Provincial Bureau of Traditional Chinese Medicine ","grant_id":"20222053"}],"categories":["Biomarker Research"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2f9rjn74cj","title":"JLP/Foxk1/N-cadherin axis fosters a partial epithelial-mesenchymal transition state in epithelial tubular cells ","description":"These data are for uncropped gels in the article \"JLP/Foxk1/N-cadherin axis fosters a partial epithelial-mesenchymal transition state in epithelial tubular cells \".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-03-10T13:48:19.931Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2f9rjn74cj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2f9rjn74cj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"maoqing","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lu","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meng","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liwen","last_name":"Qiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bingqing","last_name":"Xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chen","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Shan","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuan","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"忠平","last_name":"韦","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yujuan","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huiming","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"81800614"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82270711"},{"id":null,"name":"China Population Welfare Foundation","grant_id":"SLB-6-20210323-99"},{"id":null,"name":"Hubei Province Major Scientific and Technological Special Project","grant_id":"2019ACA137"}],"categories":["Western Blot","Gel"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7ys2r94mhv","title":"Allicin therapeutic potential in cancer: Effects on Migration, proliferation, and modulation of metastasis-associated traits in breast cancer cells ","description":"Set of pictures, images, and databases that support the assessment of the effect of allicin on the epithelial-mesenchymal transition in luminal A and triple-negative breast cancer.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-02T07:56:32.071Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7ys2r94mhv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7ys2r94mhv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Emilio","last_name":"Venegas","orcid":"0000-0002-7689-9185"},{"first_name":"Keila","last_name":"Cáceres","orcid":"0009-0005-7799-9750"},{"first_name":"Pablo ","last_name":"Ortiz","orcid":"0000-0001-9045-7052"},{"first_name":"Celia ","last_name":"González","orcid":"0000-0002-7544-4007"},{"first_name":"Alejandro ","last_name":"García","orcid":"0000-0002-7250-4641"},{"first_name":"Vida ","last_name":"ROSAS","orcid":"0000-0001-5663-852X"}],"funders":[{"id":"http://dx.doi.org/10.13039/100020149","name":"Consejo Estatal de Ciencia y Tecnología de Jalisco","grant_id":"10294-2022"}],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Metastasis","Anticancer Drug","Natural Compound","Phytopharmaceutical"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9d5499gb8x","title":"In Vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Despite remarkable clinical efficacy of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances anti-tumor immunity, and strengthens ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed that Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. In addition, deletion of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy in TNBC by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-24T14:43:28.246Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9d5499gb8x","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9d5499gb8x"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiaoqing","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9xkgn447vz","title":"In vivo CRISPR Screens Identify E3 Ligase Cop1 as a Modulator of Macrophage Infiltration and Cancer Immunotherapy Target","description":"Summary: Despite remarkable clinical efficacies of immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) in cancer treatment, ICB benefits in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC) remain limited. Through pooled in vivo CRISPR knockout (KO) screens in syngeneic TNBC mouse models, we found that inhibition of the E3 ubiquitin ligase Cop1 in cancer cells decreases the secretion of macrophage-associated chemokines, reduces tumor macrophage infiltration, enhances tumor immunity and ICB response. Transcriptomics, epigenomics, and proteomics analyses revealed Cop1 functions through proteasomal degradation of the C/ebpδ protein. Cop1 substrate Trib2 functions as a scaffold linking Cop1 and C/ebpδ, which leads to polyubiquitination of C/ebpδ. Cop1 inhibition stabilizes C/ebpδ to suppress the expression of macrophage chemoattractant genes. Our integrated approach implicates Cop1 as a target for improving cancer immunotherapy efficacy by regulating chemokine secretion and macrophage infiltration in the TNBC tumor microenvironment.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-08-23T12:28:59.397Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9xkgn447vz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9xkgn447vz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Collin","last_name":"Tokheim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Binbin","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Protein Degradation","Immunotherapy","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":141500,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":402,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":141500,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":140732,"restricted":71,"embargoed":697},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2328},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1392},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":679},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":478},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":455},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":437},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":425},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":276}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":138840},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1910},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":139},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":899},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":838},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":534},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":368}]}}